# name: test/sql/dijkstra_interrupt.test
# description: dijkstra honours DuckDB's query deadline
# group: [sql]

# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later

require routing

# A directed chain of 4000 edges (1->2->...->4001): a search from a low-numbered start vertex to
# vertex 4000 has to walk most of the chain, while a search from a start vertex near 4000 finishes
# almost immediately. dijkstra(sql, starts, ends) with both `starts` and `ends` given as lists
# takes the many-to-many driver path, which calls CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS() once per start vertex
# (third_party/pgrouting/include/dijkstra/dijkstra.hpp, via the combinations map built by
# get_combinations() in third_party/pgrouting/src/cpp_common/combinations.cpp) - one opportunity
# to observe the deadline per start vertex, in ascending start-vertex order.
#
# A single scalar END_VID (dijkstra(sql, starts, end_vid), the other many-to-one signature) is not
# usable for this test: pgRouting implements that overload by reversing the graph and swapping
# starts/ends, which collapses all requested start vertices into a single combinations-map entry
# (one departure, many destinations), so it calls CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS() exactly once for the
# whole query no matter how many start vertices are requested.
#
# DuckDB's ClientContext::InterruptCheck() (duckdb/src/main/client_context.cpp) re-reads the query
# deadline only on every 256th call to it, through a thread_local counter that only advances while
# a deadline is active; the first such call inside this query can therefore land anywhere in
# [1, 256], depending on how many deadline-checking InterruptCheck() calls earlier statements in
# this session made while a deadline was active. What that guarantees, for a query making N of
# these calls: the *last* possible deadline-reading call inside those N calls sits at some position
# >= N - 256 (checks recur every 256 calls, so if the last one before N were earlier than that,
# another one would fit before N, contradicting "last"). So if the work done by the first N - 256
# searches alone already exceeds the 100 ms deadline set below, the interrupt is guaranteed to fire
# before all N searches complete, regardless of where in [1, 256] the first check happens to land.
# That is a guarantee that this query gets aborted, not a prediction of when - in practice the
# deadline is crossed, and the interrupt fires, much earlier than after N - 256 searches (see the
# measurements below).
#
# On this chain graph, an early (low-id) start vertex's search walks nearly the whole chain, so the
# guaranteed N - 256 searches' work grows roughly with (N - 256) * N: this is why N=4000 is used
# here rather than a much thinner margin over 256. Measured directly against a release build: a
# query that performs only those first N - 256 = 3744 searches completes in ~416 ms including
# returning its rows; net of that result-emission cost, the searches themselves are estimated at
# roughly 2-3x the 100 ms deadline below, comfortably outlasting it. A debug build runs each search
# markedly slower, so the same margin only widens there. The actual observed runtime of the query
# below (which aborts long before all N searches run) is far lower than that 416 ms figure in a
# release build; in a debug build it is higher in absolute terms, since every search costs more,
# but the interrupt still fires well before a full N-search run would complete - measured over 10
# runs each: release ~0.11-0.14s, debug ~5.5-5.7s.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE big_edges AS
SELECT i AS id, i AS source, i + 1 AS target, 1.0 AS cost, 1.0 AS reverse_cost
FROM range(1, 4001) t(i);

statement ok
SET max_execution_time = 100;

statement error
SELECT count(*) FROM dijkstra(
  'SELECT id, source, target, cost, reverse_cost FROM big_edges',
  range(1, 4001)::BIGINT[], [4000]::BIGINT[]);
----
INTERRUPT Error: Interrupted!

statement ok
SET max_execution_time = 0;
